028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $A$ and $B$ be events in a sample space $S$ with a probability function $\\mathbb{P}$. Which condition guarantees that $B=A^c$ for every such $S$ and $\\mathbb{P}$?","options":["$A\\cap B=\\varnothing$","$\\mathbb{P}(A)+\\mathbb{P}(B)=1$","$A\\cap B=\\varnothing$ and $A\\cup B=S$","$A\\cap B=\\varnothing$ and $\\mathbb{P}(A)+\\mathbb{P}(B)=1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"68b1ec81bbb8901cb5d325f7","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1b_probability_theory_for_eor_complement_of_an_event_14eb8bc5_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Events $A$ and $B$ in a sample space $S$ have $\\mathbb{P}(A)=0.6$, $\\mathbb{P}(B)=0.5$ and $\\mathbb{P}(A\\cap B)=0.2$. What is $\\mathbb{P}(A^c\\cap B)$?","options":["$0.8$","$0.3$","$0.4$","$0.1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a4047477da6b36595acf7e4b","time_limit":30}]}}